WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.225
One honest slide.

2
00:00:01.225 --> 00:00:05.350
The overview simplified three
things, and at this depth they bite.

3
00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:06.963
This is design guidance.

4
00:00:06.963 --> 00:00:09.775
First, some O U's don't map cleanly.

5
00:00:09.775 --> 00:00:15.475
One may exist for a single hand-made
delegation, a G P O link, or a sync filter.

6
00:00:15.475 --> 00:00:18.363
Record those rather than
forcing them into the table.

7
00:00:18.363 --> 00:00:22.013
Second, HR codes aren't
always access boundaries.

8
00:00:22.013 --> 00:00:25.916
Convert a group only when its
membership really follows the codes,

9
00:00:25.916 --> 00:00:28.400
and give the misfits a reviewed exception.

10
00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:31.366
HR also enters actions
late or future-dated,

11
00:00:31.366 --> 00:00:35.238
and worker types go well beyond
employee and contractor.

12
00:00:35.238 --> 00:00:40.248
Third, removing the other writers can
be a project in itself: the help desk,

13
00:00:40.248 --> 00:00:45.175
self-service, Exchange's custom-attribute
commands, and O U write rights.

14
00:00:45.175 --> 00:00:48.225
Find every writer in the audit logs first.

15
00:00:48.225 --> 00:00:51.580
After Gate 2, any other
write counts as drift.
